{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:47:20.664Z","data":{"turn":35,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I have a comprehensive picture of the application. Let me create todos for each injection source that needs analysis, then systematically trace each one.\\n\\nFrom the recon report, the injection sources are:\\n1. SQL Injection: `GET /api/users?search=` - users.service.js:33 (PUBLIC)\\n2. SQL Injection: `GET /api/admin/users?search=` - admin.service.js:66 (Admin-only)\\n3. NoSQL Injection: `GET /api/gigs?tag_filter=` - gigs.service.js:41-45 (Public, possibly blocked)\\n\\n\\n4. NoSQL Injection: `GET /api/admin/reports/activity?usernames[]=` - admin.service.js:358-364 (Admin-only)\\n5. SSRF: `GET /api/integrations/import?url=` (Authenticated)\\n6. SSRF: `POST /api/webhooks/test` (Authenticated)\\n7. SSRF: `POST /api/messages/conversations/:id/link-preview` (Authenticated)\\n8. HTML Injection (Puppeteer): pdf.js:55-76 (Second-order)\\n9. HTML Injection in Email: contracts.service.js\\n10. Stored XSS in GigDetail.jsx\\n11. Unrestricted File Upload\\n12. Financial/Logic Injection webhook\\n\\nNow I'm refocusing on the specialist role scope - SQL Injection, Command Injection, LFI/RFI, SSTI, Path Traversal, and Deserialization. Looking at the exploitation queue, the injection-focused vulnerabilities are the SQL and NoSQL injections, with the Puppeteer HTML injection potentially being SSTI-like, while SSRF falls outside this analysis scope.\\n\\nI'll prioritize SQLi and command injection but also document the other injection types in the standard format, then set up analysis tasks for each source.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:47:20.664Z"}}
