 — parsed.hostname used directly in request options object\\\",\\n      \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"vulnerable\\\",\\n      \\\"mismatch_reason\\\": \\\"No restriction on destination hostname, IP range, or port. Attacker can direct the server to make GET requests to any internal Docker service or cloud metadata endpoint. Response is parsed as JSON and returned directly to the attacker.\\\",\\n      \\\"witness_payload\\\": \\\"http://mailhog:8025/api/v2/messages\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Requires any authenticated user (self-register via POST /api/auth/register). Response returned to caller as parsed JSON — full read SSRF. Internal Docker services reachable: postgres:5432, mongodb:27017, redis:6379, minio:9000, minio:9001, mailhog:8025. For non-JSON services, JSON.parse will fail but the request still reaches the target. Also try: http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ for cloud metadata.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"INJ-VULN-07\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"LFI\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"req.body.url — POST /api/webhooks/test (webhook.routes.js:50)\\\",\\n      \\\"combined_sources\\\": \\\"Single source: req.body.url\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"webhook.routes.js:48 (authenticate middleware) → webhook.routes.js:50 (const { url } = req.body) → webhook.service.js:149 (new URL(url) — parse only) → webhook.service.js:150-165 (transport.request POST sink)\\\",\\n      \\\"sink_call\\\": \\\"src/integrations/webhook.service.js:165 — transport.request({ hostname: parsed.hostname, ..., method: POST }); webhook.service.js:186 req.write(testPayload)\\\",\\n      \\\"slot_type\\\": \\\"FILE-path\\\",\\n      \\\"sanitization_observed\\\": \\\"new URL() parse at webhook.service.js:149 — validates URL syntax only, no blocklist\\\",\\n      \\\"concat_occurrences\\\": \\\"None\\\",\\n      \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"vulnerable\\\",\\n      \\\"mismatch_reason\\\": \\\"No restriction on destination. Attacker controls the POST target URL; the server issues a POST request containing a HireFlow test payload to the attacker-specified internal endpoint. HTTP status and response body are returned to the caller.\\\",\\n      \\\"witness_payload\\\": \\\"http://redis:6379/\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Requires any authenticated user. POST body contains a fixed JSON test payload (not attacker-controlled), but state-mutating side effects are possible on HTTP-speaking internal services (MinIO S3 API, MailHog, internal app endpoints). HTTP status + response body returned to caller. Combine with INJ-VULN-06 for broader internal recon.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"INJ-VULN-08\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"LFI\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"req.body.url — POST /api/messages/conversations/:id/link-preview (messaging.controller.js:125)\\\",\\n      \\\"combined_sources\\\": \\\"Single source: req.body.url\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"messaging.routes.js:14 (authenticate middleware) → messaging.controller.js:125 (var url = req.body.url) → messaging.service.js:371 (new URL() parse) → messaging.service.js:377-384 (protocol + partial hostname blocklist — bypassable) → messaging.service.js:387 (http.get / https.get sink)\\\",\\n      \\\"sink_call\\\": \\\"src/messaging/messaging.service.js:387 — client.get(url, { timeout: 5000 }, callback)\\\",\\n      \\\"slot_type\\\": \\\"FILE-path\\\",\\n      \\\"sanitization_observed\\\": \\\"Protocol check (http/https only) at messaging.service.js:377-379 — effective; Hostname blocklist (localhost, 127.0.0.1) at messaging.service.js:382-384 — bypassable\\\",\\n      \\\"concat_occurrences\\\": \\\"None\\\",\\n      \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"vulnerable\\\",\\n      \\\"mismatch_reason\\\": \\\"Blocklist covers only two of many loopback/private address representations. [::1], 0.0.0.0, 127.0.0.2, any RFC-1918 address, and Docker service hostnames (postgres, mongodb, redis, minio, mailhog) all bypass the check. DNS rebinding is also viable.\\\",\\n      \\\"witness_payload\\\": \\\"http://[::1]:8025/api/v2/messages\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Requires any authenticated user. Bypass payloads: http://[::1]:PORT, http://0.0.0.0:PORT, http://postgres:5432, http://10.0.0.1:PORT (Docker bridge typically 172.17.0.1). Response limited to 512KB (messaging.service.js:393). OG metadata (title, description, image URL) extracted and returned to caller — confirms SSRF success via returned data.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}'\",\"description\":\"Save exploitation queue with all 8 vulnerabilities\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:55:03.651Z"}}
